message.content:
{
  "133": {
    "document_id": "133",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry criticizes the Administration's Dubai Ports deal and the parent company's participation in the Arab League boycott of Israel. This is criticism of the Administration and position-taking; no benefit is claimed as delivered by the senator."
  },
  "214": {
    "document_id": "214",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "A Pearl Harbor remembrance statement. The negative clarification classifies ceremonial content (commemorations, honoring service members) as not credit-claiming."
  },
  "299": {
    "document_id": "299",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Alexander receives a 'Hero of the Taxpayer' award from Americans for Tax Reform based on his voting record. This is ceremonial (an award to the senator) and reflects position-taking/votes, not a specific delivered benefit to constituents."
  },
  "490": {
    "document_id": "490",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Webb responds to the NIE on Iran by criticizing the Bush Administration's foreign policy failures. Pure criticism/position-taking with no claimed delivered benefit."
  },
  "630": {
    "document_id": "630",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd comments on the Connecticut impact of Bush's proposed budget, praising Connecticut manufacturers and urging more submarine production. This is position-taking and advocacy regarding a proposed budget; no outcome is claimed as caused by him."
  },
  "721": {
    "document_id": "721",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement on Iraq salutes Iraqi voters and troops and criticizes the President for lacking a plan. This is position-taking and criticism of the Administration, not credit-claiming for any delivered benefit."
  },
  "773": {
    "document_id": "773",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The Senate passes a resolution honoring the late Sen. Thomas Eagleton; McCaskill praises his legacy. The negative clarification treats ceremonial content honoring other people as not credit-claiming; McCaskill claims no benefit for constituents."
  },
  "1060": {
    "document_id": "1060",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage of SCHIP reauthorization and highlights that the legislation 'includes an amendment co-sponsored by Senator Nelson' (a small business health care sense-of-the-Senate provision), with the sub-headline 'Nebraska's Senator's Small Business Health Care Amendment Included.' Under clarification (d), a provision the senator championed being included in legislation that passed a chamber counts as credit-claiming; the release frames it as his legislative accomplishment. His vote for the bill alone would be position-taking, but the claimed inclusion of his amendment triggers the credit-claiming classification."
  },
  "1086": {
    "document_id": "1086",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's negative example verbatim: Dayton applauds Senate passage of LIHEAP funding but stresses its inadequacy and notes his own four amendments were defeated. He explicitly claims no responsibility for a delivered benefit, so it is welcoming/applauding without a causal claim."
  },
  "1155": {
    "document_id": "1155",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement urges continued work toward an asbestos trust fund; he notes as background the committee's unanimous adoption of the Leahy-Hatch medical criteria amendment, but the underlying bill never passed and no trust fund or benefit was delivered. Per the negative clarification, this is position-taking and general leadership claims used as context rather than a claim tied to a specific delivered benefit; committee-level adoption is not chamber passage under clarification (d)."
  },
  "1313": {
    "document_id": "1313",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman expresses frustration at the House's refusal to take up the D.C. voting rights bill and commends others' efforts. This is position-taking and advocacy for pending legislation that has not passed; no outcome is claimed as delivered."
  },
  "1827": {
    "document_id": "1827",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline announces that provisions of Lugar's Good Samaritan Hunger Relief Act 'will be included' in the Grassley-Baucus Katrina tax relief package, and Lugar thanks the chairmen for including 'this important provision.' Under the definition and clarification (d), claiming inclusion of provisions the senator authored/championed in advancing legislation is credit-claiming as a personal legislative accomplishment, going beyond mere introduction (the negative example) because his authored provisions were actually incorporated into the committee's package."
  },
  "2003": {
    "document_id": "2003",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Clinton announces the Air Force will base a new Predator squadron in New York, but she 'hailed' the decision and said 'I am pleased that the Air Force recognizes that New York can support this additional mission' — attributing the decision to the Air Force's judgment and claiming no causal role (no request, pressure, or intervention). This directly matches the negative clarification's 'welcoming, applauding, or hailing a government decision in which the senator claims no causal role,' paralleling the Clinton NIOSH negative example."
  },
  "2143": {
    "document_id": "2143",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Murray's statement on the passing of former Washington Democratic Party chairwoman Karen Marchioro is a condolence/commemoration. The negative clarification lists ceremonial content (condolences, honoring other people) as not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3047": {
    "document_id": "3047",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release (from Sen. Craig's office) announces USDA's purchase of up to 80 million pounds of potatoes and explicitly ties it to the Idaho Congressional Delegation's March 15 letter 'requesting this action,' implying the delegation's intervention caused the agency's decision. Under clarification (c), claiming the senator's letter/request caused an agency action is credit-claiming; credit shared with the delegation still counts."
  },
  "3201": {
    "document_id": "3201",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Talent announces USDA's disaster designation for Missouri and the release states he 'worked with USDA officials to expedite the approval of the Governor's request'; it also references his earlier announcement of $1,078,500 in ECP funds. Under clarifications (a) and (c), announcing a disaster declaration benefiting his state while claiming his intervention helped expedite the agency action is credit-claiming."
  },
  "3914": {
    "document_id": "3914",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Frist comments on new job numbers by crediting Republican economic policies generally and attacking Democrats. This is position-taking/economic commentary with no specific, tangible benefit claimed as personally delivered by the senator."
  },
  "3950": {
    "document_id": "3950",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Harkin expresses hope that the new regional diabetes facility will help Iowans but claims no causal role in delivering it; the release notes he is a leader on health issues and 'recently authored the HeLP America Act,' which is background authorship of legislation that has not passed. Per the negative clarification, past authorship mentioned as background and general leadership claims not tied to a delivered benefit are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4124": {
    "document_id": "4124",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy commends President Bush's call for comprehensive immigration reform and urges turning talk into action. This is position-taking/advocacy; no benefit is claimed as delivered by the senator."
  },
  "4344": {
    "document_id": "4344",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar announces his support for the bipartisan PATRIOT Act compromise with the White House. Expressing support for a deal is position-taking; he claims no authored provision or delivered benefit as his own accomplishment."
  }
}

{
  "817": {
    "document_id": "817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release reports that a subcommittee held a hearing on the Social Security Fairness Act that Collins sponsored, and describes her testimony advocating for it. Under the negative clarification, sponsoring legislation and testifying at a hearing are advocacy/position-taking where no outcome has been delivered or claimed as caused by the senator; the bill has not passed."
  },
  "871": {
    "document_id": "871",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar questions Interior Secretary Norton about proposed budget cuts to PILT, LWCF, and Park Service rules. This is oversight and criticism of the Administration with no claimed outcome or delivered benefit, falling under the negative clarification's 'oversight or criticism without a claim of produced results' and 'hearings where no outcome is claimed as caused by the senator.'"
  },
  "1118": {
    "document_id": "1118",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Carper's statement explains his vote against the constitutional amendment banning gay marriage. Under the negative clarification, vote explanations and position-taking statements are not credit-claiming; no benefit is announced and no outcome is claimed as caused by him."
  },
  "1177": {
    "document_id": "1177",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Pryor and Chambliss call on the Air Force to reimburse unpaid subcontractors and announce they will 'continue to mount pressure.' Under the negative clarification, advocacy, requests, and pressure where no outcome is claimed as caused by the senators are not credit-claiming; no reimbursement or resolution has been delivered."
  },
  "1230": {
    "document_id": "1230",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Harkin and Hatch welcome the FDA's publication of GMP regulations, but the release explicitly notes they were the principal authors of DSHEA (which created the FDA's authority) and that 'they have been pushing the FDA to publish GMPs for more than a decade.' Under clarification (c), the release claims the senators' authorship and sustained pressure produced the agency action, framing them as responsible for a long-sought outcome — unlike merely welcoming a decision in which they claim no role."
  },
  "1320": {
    "document_id": "1320",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Hutchison announces passage of an emergency appropriations bill containing $3 billion for drought and wildfire relief and states 'I am pleased to help secure this funding.' This is an explicit first-person claim of a causal role in delivering federal disaster funds under clarification (b), matching the definition of credit-claiming."
  },
  "1747": {
    "document_id": "1747",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that 'the U.S. Senate has approved legislation by Sen. Robert C. Byrd' boosting funding for the National Guard Youth ChalleNGe program — 'Byrd's provision' in the Defense Authorization Bill, which passed the Senate 98-0. Under clarification (d), a provision the senator authored has passed a chamber, which counts as credit-claiming."
  },
  "1862": {
    "document_id": "1862",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Baucus and Tester announce $12.5 million for specific Butte, Montana military projects included in the Defense Appropriations Bill, stating they 'delivered on their promise to secure federal funding' and 'were proud to go to bat for these dollars.' Under clarifications (a) and (b), the release announces federal money benefiting their state and explicitly claims a personal causal role in securing it; shared credit between the two senators still counts."
  },
  "2026": {
    "document_id": "2026",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release announces that the Senate Judiciary Committee passed the Leahy-Specter data privacy bill, which 'now moves to the full Senate.' The codebook's clarification (d) requires that an authored bill 'has actually passed a chamber or been included in passed/adopted legislation'; committee passage is not passage by a chamber and no outcome has been delivered. This falls under the negative clarification for legislation that has not passed and delivered no outcome. I considered whether committee approval of a senator's own bill counts as an accomplishment, but the codebook's chamber-passage threshold excludes it."
  },
  "2627": {
    "document_id": "2627",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This is the codebook's own positive example: Talent announces the Senate approved 'his request' for $1.5 million for a University of Missouri facility, and university officials thank him for 'securing the money.' This is an explicit, specific claim of responsibility for delivered federal funds under clarifications (a) and (b)."
  },
  "2675": {
    "document_id": "2675",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd, Lieberman, and Simmons met with BRAC commissioners to argue against closing Sub Base New London. The release describes advocacy in an ongoing process ('until we reach the finish line and hopefully prevail our work isn't done') with no outcome claimed. Under the negative clarification, meetings and advocacy where no outcome is claimed as caused by the senator are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3271": {
    "document_id": "3271",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces unanimous Senate passage of the Financial Services Regulatory Relief Act, 'the measure authored by Idaho Senator Mike Crapo,' and describes his leadership in moving it. Under clarification (d), a bill the senator authored has actually passed a chamber, which counts as credit-claiming for a legislative accomplishment."
  },
  "3477": {
    "document_id": "3477",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Cornyn's release announces the Senate confirmation of a Texan to chair the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board and highlights his own role: 'Cornyn chaired the nominations hearing for Raul and Dinkins.' Under clarification (a), a release announcing an appointment benefiting the senator's state that is issued to associate the senator with the outcome (here, via his stated procedural role in delivering the confirmation) counts as credit-claiming. I note some ambiguity, since Cornyn did not nominate Dinkins and his quote is partly laudatory, but the framing ties him causally to the appointment."
  },
  "3501": {
    "document_id": "3501",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Tester's statement expresses disappointment that the Air Force plans to eliminate missiles at Malmstrom and pledges to 'continue to fight' and 'work toward getting another mission.' This is position-taking with no delivered benefit or claimed outcome, squarely within the negative clarification."
  },
  "3534": {
    "document_id": "3534",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Domenici credits President Bush for the gasoline price plan and calls for unified action. He claims no personal role in any delivered benefit — he is praising another actor's decision. Under the negative clarification, position-taking and applauding a government action in which the senator claims no causal role is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3560": {
    "document_id": "3560",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The Arkansas delegation, including Senator Pryor, announces $877,749 in HUD grants to Arkansas housing agencies, with a detailed list of awards. Under clarification (a), a release announcing federal grants benefiting the senator's state, issued to associate the delegation with the benefit, counts as credit-claiming even without the literal words 'I secured,' and credit shared among the delegation still counts. I noted the quotes applaud the housing agencies for seeking the funds, but the announcement itself claims association with the delivered federal money."
  },
  "3729": {
    "document_id": "3729",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Grassley announces that President Bush named Matt Dummermuth interim U.S. Attorney for the Northern District of Iowa and emphasizes that 'Dummermuth was one of three names Grassley nominated to fill the opening.' Under clarification (a), the release announces an appointment benefiting the senator's state and claims Grassley's recommendation produced it, associating him with the delivered outcome."
  },
  "3744": {
    "document_id": "3744",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces that the CDC plans to establish a field station in Hawaii, a federal facility benefiting his state, and the release notes the CDC previously 'at Senator Inouye's request established a small occupational health center at the University of Hawaii at Hilo.' Under clarification (a), the release announces a federal facility for Hawaii issued to associate the senator with the benefit, and it explicitly claims his past request caused an earlier CDC action (clarification (c))."
  },
  "3845": {
    "document_id": "3845",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Bayh's statement criticizes OPEC's production decision and promotes his energy bill (S.2025), which has not passed. Under the negative clarification, this is position-taking and advocacy for pending legislation that has delivered no outcome; no benefit is claimed as caused by him."
  },
  "4531": {
    "document_id": "4531",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Thune and Clinton announce they have introduced legislation extending child care benefits for families of fallen service members. The bill has not passed, and the references to past co-sponsored legislation are background. The negative clarification explicitly excludes 'introduction or cosponsorship of legislation that has not passed and delivered no outcome, even if past authorship is mentioned as background.'"
  }
}

{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce an agreement whose provisions were included in the Collins-Lieberman bill, but that bill was only 'introduced today' — it has not passed a chamber or been adopted, and no funds or tangible benefits have been delivered. Under the negative clarification, introduction of legislation that has not passed is position-taking/legislative process news, not credit-claiming. Clarification (d) requires actual passage or inclusion in passed/adopted legislation, which is not met."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as a column praising U.S. Marshals, the senator explicitly states: 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' This is a direct first-person claim of securing specific delivered federal funding, squarely within clarification (b) ('I helped secure')."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While much of the release summarizes Bond's positions in a Chamber of Commerce speech, it contains a specific credit claim: 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' This ties Bond (as co-author) to a passed law currently delivering quantified funds to Missouri, satisfying clarification (d) (authored/championed legislation that has passed and is delivering benefits; shared credit still counts)."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby's release ('Shelby Announces $3,510 for Homewood Fire Department') announces federal grant money flowing to an Alabama fire department, with Shelby (an Appropriations Committee member) as the announcer associating himself with the benefit. Under clarification (a), announcing federal money benefiting the senator's constituents, where the release is issued to associate the senator with the benefit, counts as credit-claiming even without the words 'I secured.'"
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Explicit credit-claiming: the release announces a $105,550 grant to the Parma Fire Department 'through Sen. DeWine's bill,' states DeWine 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' and that he 'helped secure approximately $650 million' for the program. This matches clarification (a), (b), and (d) — announced funds plus explicit claims of authorship and securing appropriations, mirroring the Talent positive example."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dole's floor statement criticizes Democrats' filibuster of judicial nominees and defends the constitutional option. It is pure position-taking and criticism of other actors with no claim of delivered funds, projects, or enacted outcomes — squarely within the negative clarification (position-taking/floor statements)."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison signs on as an original cosponsor of a just-introduced veterans benefits bill. The bill has not passed and no outcome has been delivered. The negative clarification expressly covers 'cosponsorship of legislation that has not passed and delivered no outcome.'"
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez issues a statement on pending House OCS legislation, applauding Putnam, stating opposition in current form, and threatening a filibuster. This is position-taking on pending legislation with no claimed delivered benefit or causal role in any outcome."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe announces $8.1 million for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard included in an appropriations bill that passed the Senate, noting 'This funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins.' She also announces veterans funding she supported and letters she sent requesting it. This matches clarification (a) and (b): announced federal funds for her state tied to her request, like the Talent positive example; shared credit with Collins still counts."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release is ceremonial: an essay contest inviting children to honor 'Outstanding Hoosier Dads.' The mention that parts of Bayh's Responsible Fatherhood Act were signed into law is a single background clause establishing his general leadership on fatherhood, not tied to any specific delivered benefit in this release. Per the negative clarification, ceremonial content and general leadership claims used as context are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords' statement explains his vote for the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq and criticizes the Administration's war policy. This is a vote explanation/position-taking with no claim of any delivered benefit — explicitly covered by the negative clarification."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline and text announce 'SENATE ADOPTS NELSON AMENDMENT' — his amendment blocking USDA from allowing Japanese beef imports until Japan reciprocates passed 72-26 as part of the FY2006 Agriculture Appropriations bill. Under clarification (d), an amendment the senator authored that has actually passed a chamber counts as credit-claiming; the release frames it as 'a victory for Nebraska's ranchers.'"
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy introduces his 'Medicare for All' bill and delivers an extended floor statement on the health care crisis. This is introduction of legislation that has not passed plus position-taking — exactly the Kennedy negative example pattern. No funds, projects, or enacted outcomes are claimed."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Levin's release announces a PSI hearing but goes beyond mere oversight: it claims concrete investigative results produced by the Subcommittee — 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight, collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415%... to $31 million in 2006' and levies 'jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' The clarification states that claims of oversight or investigative results with concrete claimed outcomes count as credit-claiming."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kyl co-sponsors an amendment to the Budget Resolution calling for 2,000 new Border Patrol agents. No passage or delivered outcome is claimed — it is co-sponsorship/advocacy for pending legislation, which the negative clarification excludes ('introduction or cosponsorship of legislation that has not passed and delivered no outcome')."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release centers on Cornyn's letter asking Secretary Chertoff to consider the IBWC levee proposal — advocacy/a request with no claimed outcome (negative clarification: 'advocacy, requests, letters... where no outcome is claimed as caused by the senator'). Background mentions that Cornyn 'added an amendment' requiring only a report to Congress and 'sent a letter' requesting maximum appropriations; neither claims a delivered tangible benefit (no funding secured, and the reporting requirement is not a concrete benefit valued by constituents). I considered whether the added amendment counts under clarification (d), but the release claims no passed law or delivered funds, so it remains advocacy."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release primarily introduces new FMAP legislation (which alone would be 0), it contains an explicit claim of a past delivered benefit: Murkowski 'helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount.' This is more than 'past authorship mentioned as background' — it is a specific, quantified, delivered benefit claimed as caused by her, satisfying clarification (b)/(d). I weigh this against the negative clarification on introductions, but the concrete $34.11 million delivered-funds claim tips it to credit-claiming."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott announces FEMA moving 300 travel trailers and 100 mobile homes to Mississippi for Katrina victims, and the release explicitly frames the action as following his intervention: 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport of available trailers.' This matches clarification (c) (claiming the senator's request caused an agency/presidential action) and (a) (announced tangible benefits for his state)."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison questions officials at a subcommittee hearing, receives assurances on BRAC, and expresses support for the Army's 'Grow the Force' request, saying she is 'encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood.' She claims no causal role in the funding — this is oversight/hearing activity and support expressed without a claimed produced result, per the negative clarification."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the release's purpose is urging conferees to retain the provision, it prominently claims a completed legislative accomplishment: 'Last month, the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Durbin' to end the Patriot Penalty, repeatedly calling it 'his legislation.' Under clarification (d), an amendment the senator authored that has actually passed a chamber counts as credit-claiming even though final enactment is pending; the release frames Bayh as responsible for the Senate-passed provision benefiting Guard and Reserve members."
  }
}
